1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,280
The Last Race at Kranji:

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:05,900
182 Years of the Singapore Turf Club

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,375
On the 5th of October 2024,
about 10,000 people went to

4
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:15,425
Kranji for the last race meeting
of the Singapore Turf Club.

5
00:00:15,425 --> 00:00:20,193
The last race of the day was
the 100th Singapore Gold Cup,

6
00:00:20,273 --> 00:00:26,125
and when it was over, horse racing
in Singapore ended after 182 years.

7
00:00:26,125 --> 00:00:29,449
In that time the club
raced on three courses:

8
00:00:29,529 --> 00:00:33,762
an open plain off Serangoon
Road that is now Farrer Park,

9
00:00:33,842 --> 00:00:37,700
a larger course at Bukit
Timah, and finally Kranji.

10
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:41,899
Each move took the horses further
from the town, and each of the

11
00:00:41,979 --> 00:00:46,325
three sites has since been taken
back for the city's other needs.

12
00:00:46,325 --> 00:00:51,198
The Singapore Sporting Club was
founded on the 4th of October

13
00:00:51,278 --> 00:00:55,825
1842 by William Henry Macleod
Read, a Scottish merchant.

14
00:00:55,825 --> 00:01:00,259
Its members laid out a course and
built a grandstand on swampy land

15
00:01:00,339 --> 00:01:04,639
off Serangoon Road, and in December
the stewards announced in The

16
00:01:04,719 --> 00:01:09,300
Singapore Free Press that the course
and stand were almost finished.

17
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:14,982
The first race meeting was held on
the 23rd and 25th of February 1843,

18
00:01:15,062 --> 00:01:20,000
timed for the anniversary of
the founding of the settlement.

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,844
Read himself won the first Singapore Cup,

20
00:01:23,924 --> 00:01:26,700
and the prize money was $150.

21
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:29,472
For the next 90 years the races were

22
00:01:29,552 --> 00:01:32,800
held on this plain, usually twice a year.

23
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:37,525
Between meetings the open
ground was used for recreation.

24
00:01:37,525 --> 00:01:42,261
The club was renamed the
Singapore Turf Club in 1924,

25
00:01:42,341 --> 00:01:44,987
and that year it ran the first

26
00:01:45,067 --> 00:01:48,975
Singapore Gold Cup,
which was worth $1,600.

27
00:01:48,975 --> 00:01:52,150
The town was growing around the course.

28
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:56,970
In 1927 the club sold the land to
the Singapore Improvement Trust,

29
00:01:57,050 --> 00:01:59,271
the colonial housing authority,

30
00:01:59,351 --> 00:02:04,325
and bought part of a rubber estate
at Bukit Timah for a new course.

31
00:02:04,325 --> 00:02:09,596
The old ground was renamed Farrer
Park in 1935, after Roland Farrer,

32
00:02:09,676 --> 00:02:13,925
who had been President of
the Municipal Commissioners.

33
00:02:13,925 --> 00:02:18,450
Race Course Road, which ran
beside it, still keeps the name.

34
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:22,712
The new racecourse at Bukit
Timah was opened on the

35
00:02:22,792 --> 00:02:27,475
15th of April 1933 by the
Governor, Sir Cecil Clementi.

36
00:02:27,475 --> 00:02:30,743
The Straits Times reported
his speech under the

37
00:02:30,823 --> 00:02:34,662
headline "Sir Cecil Looks
Into The Future", and a crowd

38
00:02:34,742 --> 00:02:38,375
of about 5,000 filled the
new grandstand and lawns.

39
00:02:38,375 --> 00:02:41,380
The course was far
larger than the old one,

40
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:44,975
and it was the club's home
for the next 66 years.

41
00:02:44,975 --> 00:02:48,800
Singapore was one stop
on a racing circuit.

42
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:53,871
From 1896 the Turf Club belonged
to the Malayan Racing Association,

43
00:02:53,951 --> 00:02:58,254
together with the clubs at
Penang, Ipoh and Kuala Lumpur,

44
00:02:58,334 --> 00:03:04,100
and horses and jockeys from all four
clubs raced at one another's meetings.

45
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:08,050
Racing stopped during
the Japanese Occupation.

46
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:13,043
It came back on the 15th of
November 1947, when more than 10,000

47
00:03:13,123 --> 00:03:18,750
people went to Bukit Timah for the
first day of the re-opening meeting.

48
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:24,150
The Sunday Times called it a "record
crowd in Malayan turf history".

49
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:27,879
The day before, The Straits
Times reported that ticket

50
00:03:27,959 --> 00:03:32,041
sales for the club's big sweep
had taken the total pool for

51
00:03:32,121 --> 00:03:36,000
a meeting past $1 million
for the first time in Malaya.

52
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,808
The club introduced Sunday racing in 1959,

53
00:03:39,888 --> 00:03:44,425
and in 1960 it opened its
meetings to the public.

54
00:03:44,425 --> 00:03:49,235
The sweeps that the club
ran alongside its races were

55
00:03:49,315 --> 00:03:54,217
a story of their own: in
1933 a single $5 sweep ticket

56
00:03:54,297 --> 00:03:59,015
won $54,340 at the Gold Cup
meeting, and three years

57
00:03:59,095 --> 00:04:03,800
later the Gold Cup itself
carried stakes of $5,000.

58
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,672
How those sweeps became
Singapore's national lottery is

59
00:04:07,752 --> 00:04:11,775
told in The Singapore Sweep:
How the Lottery Went Legal.

60
00:04:11,775 --> 00:04:15,397
The Gold Cup was one of
the club's richest races,

61
00:04:15,477 --> 00:04:18,650
and its prize money shows how racing grew.

62
00:04:18,650 --> 00:04:21,561
The first Gold Cup in

63
00:04:21,641 --> 00:04:24,775
1924 was worth $1,600.

64
00:04:24,775 --> 00:04:28,758
By 1936 the stakes were $5,000,

65
00:04:28,838 --> 00:04:33,950
and in 1949 the race was worth $14,000.

66
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:38,553
It reached $50,000 in 1961,

67
00:04:38,633 --> 00:04:41,328
$100,000 in 1977

68
00:04:41,408 --> 00:04:45,050
and $500,000 in 1990.

69
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:50,739
When the club moved to Kranji the
prize was raised to $1 million,

70
00:04:50,819 --> 00:04:55,700
and a one-off increase took
it to $1.5 million in 2003.

71
00:04:55,700 --> 00:05:00,252
In later years it was
set at $1.35 million,

72
00:05:00,332 --> 00:05:04,425
and by 2019 it was back at $1 million.

73
00:05:04,425 --> 00:05:07,690
For the last running in 2024 the

74
00:05:07,770 --> 00:05:11,325
prize was raised to $1.38 million.

75
00:05:11,325 --> 00:05:14,509
These figures are in the
dollars of each year,

76
00:05:14,589 --> 00:05:19,200
so the rise is much smaller once
inflation is taken into account.

77
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:23,932
By the late 1980s the land at
Bukit Timah was wanted for housing,

78
00:05:24,012 --> 00:05:28,675
and a new site was found at
Kranji, in the north of the island.

79
00:05:28,675 --> 00:05:32,084
The last race at Bukit Timah was

80
00:05:32,164 --> 00:05:35,325
run on the 25th of July 1999.

81
00:05:35,325 --> 00:05:40,974
Six days later, on the 31st of July,
Kranji opened to the racing public,

82
00:05:41,054 --> 00:05:45,191
and The New Paper reported
that only 5,000 race-goers

83
00:05:45,271 --> 00:05:50,125
were allowed into the $500
million complex for its first day.

84
00:05:50,125 --> 00:05:54,078
The new course had a grandstand
for 30,000 people and

85
00:05:54,158 --> 00:05:57,806
41 floodlight masts, and
on the 25th of September

86
00:05:57,886 --> 00:06:01,610
1999 it held Singapore's
first night race meeting,

87
00:06:01,690 --> 00:06:05,875
which was interrupted when
part of the lighting failed.

88
00:06:05,875 --> 00:06:10,182
The course was officially
opened on the 4th of March 2000

89
00:06:10,262 --> 00:06:15,185
with the first Singapore Airlines
International Cup, a $3 million

90
00:06:15,265 --> 00:06:19,575
race that was won by a horse
trained in Singapore, Ouzo.

91
00:06:19,575 --> 00:06:23,671
The International Cup
brought horses from overseas

92
00:06:23,751 --> 00:06:27,425
to Kranji until it was
discontinued in 2016.

93
00:06:27,425 --> 00:06:30,625
Crowds fell in the years that followed.

94
00:06:30,625 --> 00:06:36,028
Average attendance on a race
day dropped from about 11,000

95
00:06:36,108 --> 00:06:41,606
in 2010 to about 6,000 in 2019,
and after racing resumed in

96
00:06:41,686 --> 00:06:47,075
2022 following the Covid-19
pandemic, it was about 2,600.

97
00:06:47,075 --> 00:06:51,028
The Tote Board's chief
executive linked the decline to

98
00:06:51,108 --> 00:06:54,986
the opening of the casinos
in 2010 and to competition

99
00:06:55,066 --> 00:06:58,950
from other sports, such as
Formula One and football.

100
00:06:58,950 --> 00:07:02,528
On the 5th of June 2023
the Singapore Turf Club,

101
00:07:02,608 --> 00:07:06,492
the Ministry of Finance and
the Ministry of National

102
00:07:06,572 --> 00:07:09,925
Development announced
that racing would end.

103
00:07:09,925 --> 00:07:13,855
The club would hold its
last race meeting in October

104
00:07:13,935 --> 00:07:17,942
2024 and hand the Kranji
site, of about 120 hectares,

105
00:07:18,022 --> 00:07:22,106
back to the government by
March 2027, so that it could

106
00:07:22,186 --> 00:07:26,350
be planned for public housing,
leisure and other uses.

107
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:31,056
The Second Minister for Finance,
Indranee Rajah, cited the fall in

108
00:07:31,136 --> 00:07:35,850
attendance and pointed to a decline
in racing in other countries.

109
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:39,356
About 350 staff were to
be retrenched in phases,

110
00:07:39,436 --> 00:07:44,136
with help to find new jobs, and
owners and trainers were offered

111
00:07:44,216 --> 00:07:48,325
support to maintain their
horses or send them overseas.

112
00:07:48,325 --> 00:07:53,450
The last meeting was held
on the 5th of October 2024.

113
00:07:53,450 --> 00:07:58,472
The prize money for the day
was raised, and the 100th Grand

114
00:07:58,552 --> 00:08:04,000
Singapore Gold Cup, over 2,000
metres, was worth $1.38 million.

115
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,225
It was won by Smart Star, in
front of a crowd of about 10,000.

116
00:08:09,225 --> 00:08:12,436
Farrer Park is still an open sports field,

117
00:08:12,516 --> 00:08:15,650
and Race Course Road runs past its edge.

118
00:08:15,650 --> 00:08:18,939
The old Bukit Timah
course became Turf City,

119
00:08:19,019 --> 00:08:23,915
a shopping and leisure centre in
the former racecourse buildings,

120
00:08:23,995 --> 00:08:26,675
until it closed at the end of 2023.

121
00:08:26,675 --> 00:08:32,012
In May 2024 the Urban Redevelopment
Authority set out plans

122
00:08:32,092 --> 00:08:36,418
for a 176-hectare estate
on and around the site,

123
00:08:36,498 --> 00:08:43,200
with about 15,000 to 20,000 public
and private homes over 20 to 30 years.

124
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:45,672
Some of the old racecourse buildings,

125
00:08:45,752 --> 00:08:50,637
including the North Grandstand, are
being studied for keeping and reuse,

126
00:08:50,717 --> 00:08:54,086
and the estate will be
served by two MRT stations,

127
00:08:54,166 --> 00:08:57,200
one of them on the
future Cross Island Line.

128
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:02,093
At the National Day Rally in August
2025, Prime Minister Lawrence

129
00:09:02,173 --> 00:09:07,066
Wong said that the Kranji site
would be used for about 14,000 new

130
00:09:07,146 --> 00:09:12,425
public and private homes, with the
first expected in about ten years.

131
00:09:12,425 --> 00:09:18,641
Where it fits in the bigger story: The Turf
Club was one of Singapore's oldest clubs,

132
00:09:18,721 --> 00:09:22,425
founded only 23 years after
the settlement itself.

133
00:09:22,425 --> 00:09:25,743
Each of its moves, from
the edge of town to Bukit

134
00:09:25,823 --> 00:09:30,321
Timah and then to Kranji, came as
the city grew around the course,

135
00:09:30,401 --> 00:09:33,175
and each site has gone on to other uses.

136
00:09:33,175 --> 00:09:36,062
Farrer Park is a sports field among flats,

137
00:09:36,142 --> 00:09:40,582
and Bukit Timah and Kranji are
being planned as housing estates,

138
00:09:40,662 --> 00:09:43,902
so the names Race Course
Road and Turf City are

139
00:09:43,982 --> 00:09:47,725
among the few reminders that
horses once raced there.
